38,2,,status,Mizuki,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":4,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
39,2,,status,Yumi,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
40,2,1,talk,Rin,,"Wow, the pilot floor takes way too long! Four to nine minutes every single morning just to start working? And those unplanned shuffles sound like a total nightmare—two weeks of boxes! We need something that doesn't waste our time.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"The financial side is equally concerning. We are billed by area, not headcount, meaning empty space is a waste. Furthermore, every single desk move costs 6,200 yen, which adds up quickly if we change layouts often.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,1,talk,Mizuki,,"It seems we have a conflict between fairness and efficiency. The survey shows daylight is a major complaint, yet the plan leaves some people in the dark. Also, new joiners benefit greatly from overhearing organic conversations.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,1,talk,Shion,,"The physical data is clear: the area by the printer is significantly louder, and our equipment requires days of fine-tuning to be ergonomic. A layout that forces frequent moves or ignores noise will be a failure.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,1,talk,Yumi,,"That's so interesting! I found out that sitting near each other really helps us communicate—it even replaced those long handover notes! But the badge records show we arrive at totally different times, so first-come-first-served wouldn't be fair.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,2,talk,Rin,,"@Yumi, if we arrive at different times, then a ""first-come"" system is just a race! That sounds stressful. And @Shion, if it takes days to fix the chairs, we definitely can't be moving all the time!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Yumi"",""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Rin is correct. Between the per-desk relocation costs and the time lost to setup, any system involving frequent movement is financially and operationally inefficient. We should avoid layouts that require constant shuffling.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Rin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,2,talk,Mizuki,,"@Toshio, I agree. If we want to be fair, we can't rely on arrival times, and we can't ignore that some seats are objectively worse due to the lack of daylight. We need a stable, transparent system.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,2,talk,Shion,,"Exactly. If we prioritize a ""flexible"" system, we are essentially choosing to ignore the noise pollution by the printer and the ergonomic struggle of resetting our equipment daily. Stability is the only logical way to ensure comfort.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,2,talk,Yumi,,"I agree with everyone! If we move too much, we lose that great organic communication I found, and it sounds like it would be a headache for our bodies and our budget too. We need a plan that keeps us close!","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,3,talk,Rin,,"So, moving around sounds like a bad idea! It wastes time in the morning, messes up our chairs, and costs money. We should definitely pick something where we don't have to pack boxes every few weeks!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"I agree. Given the fixed costs of the floor area and the specific charges for each network change, a stable arrangement is the most fiscally responsible choice. We cannot afford frequent, unplanned, or rotating changes.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,3,talk,Mizuki,,"If we want to avoid the ""race"" for the best seats and ensure that the lack of daylight is handled fairly, we can't leave it to chance. We need a system that is both stable and transparent.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,3,talk,Shion,,"It is clear that any option involving movement is illogical. We cannot sacrifice ergonomic stability and acoustic focus for the sake of variety. A fixed, permanent assignment is the only way to guarantee a usable environment.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,3,talk,Yumi,,"I totally agree! If we have fixed spots, we can make sure we're sitting near the people we need to talk to, and nobody has to worry about arriving early just to get a good desk!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,2,4,talk,Rin,,I'm all for it! Let's go with something that keeps us in one place so we can just start our day and stay happy. I'm ready to vote for a fixed plan!,"{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,2,4,talk,Toshio,,I concur. A fixed arrangement minimizes both the financial drain of relocation fees and the operational waste of setup time. It is the only choice that aligns with our budget and efficiency needs.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
57,2,4,talk,Mizuki,,"I agree. A fixed system allows us to openly discuss who sits where, ensuring the lack of daylight is distributed fairly rather than by luck or arrival time. It is the most transparent and stable approach.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,2,4,talk,Shion,,"The evidence is overwhelming. To ignore the noise levels and the time required for ergonomic adjustment would be a failure of logic. A fixed layout is the only way to ensure a professional, focused environment.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
59,2,4,talk,Yumi,,I'm with you all! Let's pick the option that lets us stay close and keep our communication flowing without any morning stress. I'm voting for fixed desks!,"{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
